findings.body:
The diff changes `if (!isOrgContext)` to `if (isOrgContext)` in `buildInviteLink`. The original code sends regular teams to `/teams?token=` and org contexts to `/signup?token=...&callbackUrl=<gettingStarted>`. That matches the PR description. After the flip, regular teams get the signup link and org sub-teams get the `/teams` link.

The service is shared. The tRPC `viewer.teams.createInvite` handler used by `MemberInvitationModal` calls it too, so the web UI's invite links change as well, not just the new API endpoint.

::diff{file="packages/features/ee/teams/services/teamService.ts" lines="104-109"}

::suggestion{file="packages/features/ee/teams/services/teamService.ts" lines="106-106"}
    if (!isOrgContext) {

Revert the condition to `!isOrgContext`. Then add a unit or E2E assertion that a regular team's `inviteLink` starts with `/teams?token=` and an org sub-team's starts with `/signup?token=`, so this can't flip silently again.

The diff changes the returned `token` from the generated random value to `newToken.identifier`. The `identifier` is always `invite-link-for-teamId-${teamId}`, so it is a constant per team and not a secret. Consequences:

- Every call returns the same `token` for a team. The E2E test "should create a new invite on each request" asserts `token` is not equal to the first call's token and fails.
- `data.token` no longer appears in `data.inviteLink`, which is built from the random `token` variable. The test "should create a team invite" asserts `inviteLink` contains `token` and fails.
- The tRPC `createInvite` return value changes the same way.

::diff{file="packages/features/ee/teams/services/teamService.ts" lines="87-101"}

::suggestion{file="packages/features/ee/teams/services/teamService.ts" lines="98-101"}
    return {
      token,
      inviteLink: await TeamService.buildInviteLink(token, isOrganizationOrATeamInOrganization),
    };

Keep the `await` on `create` (no need to bind `newToken`); return the random `token` you generated.

The controller decorates `createInvite` with `@Roles("TEAM_MEMBER")`. `RolesGuard` allows any role at or above the minimum, and `TEAM_MEMBER` is the lowest role, so a regular team member passes the check. The PR description says the endpoint requires `TEAM_ADMIN`, and the sibling org controller uses `@Roles("TEAM_ADMIN")`.

Attack scenario: any member of a team can call the endpoint and get a join token and link that onboards new users into the team. The member-403 E2E test would fail here, since a member gets a 200.

::diff{file="apps/api/v2/src/modules/teams/invite/controllers/teams-invite.controller.ts" lines="31-33"}

::suggestion{file="apps/api/v2/src/modules/teams/invite/controllers/teams-invite.controller.ts" lines="32-32"}
  @Roles("TEAM_ADMIN")

With this change the "non-admin member → 403" test should pass.

The route is `/v2/teams/:teamId` with no `orgId` param. In `RolesGuard.checkUserRoleAccess`, the team-only branch (`teamId` set, `orgId` absent) requires a `TeamMembership` row and throws 403 if there is none. The branch that allows org ADMIN/OWNER access to team roles only runs when both `teamId` and `orgId` are present, which this route never supplies.

So an org admin who manages a sub-team without a direct team membership gets 403. The PR says the endpoint works for org sub-teams, but that only holds for users who are explicitly team admins. This may be intended, but it isn't tested and doesn't match the claim. Confirm the intended access model, and either document it or add an org-aware route.

::diff{file="apps/api/v2/src/modules/teams/invite/controllers/teams-invite.controller.ts" lines="30-34"}
